Two upstream quirks a consistency check will flag, both pre-existing and both
harmless: eval2/manifest.ttl defines :eval-assign-01 but does not list it in
mf:entries and does not ship its files, and eval/manifest.ttl has
:eval-bnodes-04 commented out. Neither harness reads either.

Two harnesses drive this one snapshot, because there are two questions.

  scoreboard.json / expected-pass.json
      w3c.harness.test.ts, in this package. Does the *parser* conform? Covers
      syntax/, wellformed/ and stratification/ — a parser and two analyses,
      which is all `packages/srl` is. The eval categories are not here: they
      need an executor, a store and graph comparison, none of which belong in a
      browser-usable parse-and-analyse package.

  library-scoreboard.json / library-expected-pass.json
      packages/api/test/lib/w3cRulesSuite.harness.test.ts. Can the *library*
      hold each entry, hand it back unchanged, and judge it? Covers every
      category, by seeding them as rule sets and Tests
      (packages/api/src/lib/w3cRulesSuite/) and running them through the
      ordinary test runner — the same thing a user sees on the Tests screen.
      See docs/guides/testing-and-conformance.md.

The api harness writes its two files here rather than in its own package so
every category's score is read in one place. Refreshing the pin means running
both and reconciling both baselines. That harness also asserts the per-category
entry counts outright, so a refresh that changes the suite's size fails loudly
rather than scoring against a shorter suite.

manifest-sparql-rl.ttl is the suite's own index of the six manifests. Nothing
reads it — both harnesses name their categories — but it is what says which
directories are part of the suite, so a refresh that adds a seventh is visible
in the diff.
